---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Q1 = `score ← 74
IF(score ≥ 70)
{
  DISPLAY("Pass")
}
ELSE
{
  DISPLAY("Fail")
}
DISPLAY("Done")`;

const Q2 = `let temp = 15;

if (temp > 20)
{
  console.log("Warm");
}
else
{
  console.log("Cold");
}
console.log("Reading complete");`;

const Q3 = `a ← 5
b ← 5
DISPLAY(a = b)`;

const Q5 = `let count = 3;

if (count = 5)
{
  console.log("Five");
}`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Question Set 2</h1>

        <h4 class="text-sn-theme">
          Six questions &middot; 11 minutes &middot; cumulative
        </h4>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <section id="objective">
          <h2>What This Is</h2>

          <p>
            Five exam-style questions covering Activity 1.1 through 1.12. This
            set is weighted toward <b>booleans and selection</b>, but it is{" "}
            <b>cumulative</b> &mdash; two of these five are about material from
            Set 1.
          </p>

          <p>
            Same drill as last time. <b>Set a timer for 11 minutes.</b> Work them
            out on paper; do not run the code. Mark each answer <b>sure</b> or{" "}
            <b>unsure</b>. Guess rather than leave a blank &mdash; the AP exam
            has no penalty for a wrong answer.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>One of these five is written in exam notation rather than
            JavaScript, and one mixes the two deliberately.</b> That is not a
            trick. The multiple-choice section is written in reference-sheet
            notation, and Question 3 is where the two notations disagree most.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="questions">
          <h2>The Questions</h2>

          <p>
            Four have exactly one correct answer, one asks you to select{" "}
            <i>all</i> that apply, and the last is about data or the impact of
            computing rather than about code. <b>Thirteen of the exam's seventy
            questions look like that last one</b>, so it is on every set from
            here on.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Question 1</h3>

          <p>What does the following program display?</p>

          <Code
            code={Q1}
            frame="code"
            lang="txt"
            title="Question 1 — exam notation"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />

          <ul>
            <li>(A) <code>Pass</code></li>
            <li>(B) <code>Fail</code></li>
            <li>(C) <code>Pass Done</code></li>
            <li>(D) <code>Fail Done</code></li>
          </ul>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Question 2</h3>

          <p>What does the following code display?</p>

          <Code
            code={Q2}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="Question 2"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />

          <ul>
            <li>(A) <code>Warm</code> then <code>Reading complete</code></li>
            <li>(B) <code>Cold</code> then <code>Reading complete</code></li>
            <li>(C) <code>Cold</code> only</li>
            <li>(D) <code>Warm</code> and <code>Cold</code> and <code>Reading complete</code></li>
          </ul>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Question 3</h3>

          <p>What does the following program display?</p>

          <Code
            code={Q3}
            frame="code"
            lang="txt"
            title="Question 3 — exam notation"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />

          <ul>
            <li>(A) <code>5</code></li>
            <li>(B) <code>true</code></li>
            <li>(C) <code>false</code></li>
            <li>(D) Nothing. The program has an error.</li>
          </ul>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Question 4 &mdash; Select all that apply</h3>

          <p>
            A variable <code>age</code> holds the number <code>16</code>. Which
            of the following expressions evaluate to <code>true</code>?
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>(A) <code>age &gt;= 16</code></li>
            <li>(B) <code>age !== 16</code></li>
            <li>(C) <code>age &lt; 18</code></li>
            <li>(D) <code>age === "16"</code></li>
            <li>(E) <code>age &gt; 15</code></li>
          </ul>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Question 5</h3>

          <p>
            A student writes the following JavaScript and is surprised by what
            happens. What is wrong with it?
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Q5}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="Question 5"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />

          <ul>
            <li>(A) Nothing. It correctly displays nothing, because 3 is not 5.</li>
            <li>
              (B) The condition assigns 5 to <code>count</code> instead of
              comparing, so the branch runs.
            </li>
            <li>(C) <code>count</code> should have been declared with <code>const</code>.</li>
            <li>(D) The <code>if</code> is missing an <code>else</code>.</li>
          </ul>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Question 6 &mdash; Data and Impact</h3>

          <p>A student saves a spreadsheet of student grades using lossy compression to save space. What is the problem?</p>

          <ul>
            <li>(A) Some of the grade data cannot be recovered</li>
            <li>(B) Lossy compression only works on images</li>
            <li>(C) The file will be larger, not smaller</li>
            <li>(D) Nothing; lossy compression is always safe</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>
            Six answers, each marked <b>sure</b> or <b>unsure</b>, plus how far
            you got when the timer went off.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>The sure-but-wrong answers are the most useful thing in this
            submission.</b> An unsure-and-wrong answer means you knew to slow
            down. A sure-and-wrong answer means something you believe is not
            true, and that is what a question set is for.
          </p>

          <p>
            Worth <b>2 points</b>, graded on completion.
          </p>
        </section>

        <h1
          class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
        >
          Start the Timer
        </h1>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
